## R-D1 â€” AT-001.05 settlement: CONFIRMED RED

The executor found the onboarding import reads the shipped stub (`stubGithubStatsFor`; the stored
summary's own text says no GitHub API was called to produce it) and settled the id red at
integration, recording both readings in `verify-first.md`.

**Ruling: red is correct.** The criterion's words are "the imported public stats ... observably
populated"; at the tier whose meaning is "proved against the real stack", a green resting on a
vendor read that a shipped stub answered would be exactly the false green D6 exists to kill. The
decomposition's cross-contract ratifies the stub as acceptable SHIPPED BEHAVIOUR â€” that governs
the loop tier's green, not a live-tier claim. Same doctrine as gate-1 rulings 7 and 9. The
declaration stands red with its exact kind.

## R-D2 â€” ten integration bodies instead of six: ACCEPTED

Every id that opens a world needs an integration registration or the run fails in an
undeclarable shape; three ids (.02, .03, .04) are refused by the pending proxy in exactly the
declarable shape and need no body. This is a correct consequence of the one-result-per-id
invariant D5 preserves; the plan under-enumerated, the machinery decided, no change is needed.

## R-D3 â€” the registration divergence handle: CONFIRMED, no contract shape change

The live adapter returns a handle that names the account and holds NO session (the live stack
under email confirmations issues none at registration), and every session-taking operation
refuses such a handle by name. The alternative â€” minting a session the live stack never issued â€”
would let a body proceed on fabricated evidence; the chosen design's failure direction is a
false red. That is the doctrine of gate-1 ruling 1 applied at the suite layer. The shared SUT
contract keeps its shape.

## R-D4 â€” the added dev dependency (@supabase/supabase-js): ACCEPTED for the draft

Gate-1 ruling 7 requires a real client and the repository ships none, so the dependency is the
ruling's own consequence. The load mechanism (a specifier tsc does not resolve, with a declared
client surface) is accepted as draft design and named as a risk area for review; whatever the
review finds there is ruled on its merits.

## R-D5 â€” AT-001.04's refusal name: ACCEPTED; plan table annotated

The automatic refusal truly and exactly names `sut.accounts.registerWithProvider` (the Google
half â€” the criterion names both providers), which differs from the reason the plan's table gave
(the GitHub-link clause). Both dependencies are real; the manifest must carry the name the code
actually produces, and it is analysis-derived, which is what gate-1 ruling 10 demands. The
plan's table cell is annotated to match the record.

