 129: ## Slice 2 (suite, declarations, process text) â€” reader terra, 6 findings
 130: 
 131: ### S2-1 â€” ACCEPT, CONVERGED with flash F2 (critical, _integration.ts:399)
 132: 
 133: > "AT-001.12 unconditionally waits 135 seconds despite the suite's 30-second Vitest timeout."
 134: 
 135: Verified: `testTimeout: 30_000` (vitest.config.ts:18); the runner passes exactly that config
 136: with no override (runner.ts:1344-1363); at00112 waits `SLOT_JWT_EXPIRY_MS + 15_000` = 135 s,
 137: and at00113 waits up to 150 s (flash's extension of the same defect, F2). Both declared-green
 138: time-based bodies would time out red â€” the declared integration green cannot occur.
 139: 
 140: **Remedy:** a scoped timeout raise for the integration bodies that wait out real time â€” either a
 141: per-body timeout at registration or a tier-scoped `testTimeout` the runner passes only at
 142: integration. Two constraints: the loop tier stays at 30 s, and the raised value still bounds a
 143: runaway. The goal-phase `--expect` run is the proof.
 144: 
 145: ### S2-2 â€” ACCEPT, CONVERGED with flash F1 (critical, _live.ts:338)
 146: 
 147: > "Logout deletes the cached token before AT-001.12 sends its revoked-session write."
 148: 
 149: Verified: `signOut` ends with `sessions.delete(session.sessionId)` (_live.ts:338); at00112 then
 150: calls `createOrganization(session, â€¦)`, which throws client-side in `tokensOf` (_live.ts:151-162)
 151: before any HTTP request. The revocation clause is never measured against the live stack, and the
 152: body cannot reach green.
 153: 
 154: **Remedy:** `signOut` keeps the cached tokens; the post-logout write sends the revoked token to
 155: the live stack, and the stack's refusal is the measured fact. R-D3's divergence handle is
 156: untouched â€” it covers handles that never held a session, which is a different case from a
 157: session that existed and was revoked. If the live stack ACCEPTS the revoked-but-unexpired
 158: bearer token, the body goes red and that red is a true product signal: investigated as a defect
 159: first, per the plan's step-7 doctrine, never a declaration bent to the run.
 160: 
 161: ### S2-3 â€” ACCEPT, with a verify-first arm (high, _integration.ts:190)
 162: 
 163: > "AT-001.01's \"atomicity\" arm omits `acknowledgmentTextVersion`, which the edge validator
 164: > rejects before the database transaction starts."
 165: 
 166: Verified: the arm at _integration.ts:190 sends no `acknowledgmentTextVersion`; a validator
 167: refusal precedes the transaction, so "zero rows left" is trivially true and mid-transaction
 168: atomicity is unproved.
 169: 
 170: **Remedy, in order:** the executor inspects the deployed complete-signup function. If an input
 171: exists that passes upfront validation and fails inside the transaction after earlier writes,
 172: the arm drives that input and asserts zero rows. If no such input is drivable from outside,
 173: the arm stays, its comment is rewritten to claim exactly what it proves (the deployed path
 174: refuses a completion without acknowledgment and leaves no rows), and the item record plus the
 175: audit claim checklist state that in-transaction rollback is not externally drivable on the
 176: deployed surface. The green then rests on the full-outcome positive oracle plus the refusal
 177: arm, which is the migrated transcript check gate-1 ruling 9 conditioned the green on. No
 178: fault-injection seam is added to shipped code for this.
 179: 
