 602:  *
 603:  * REQ-001 binds exactly one system-under-test key, `accounts`, and thirty-seven acceptance ids drive
 604:  * it. A verdict at the key level therefore says one word about thirty-seven different questions: the
 605:  * live adapter genuinely backs a sign-in and genuinely cannot perform an OAuth consent round trip,
 606:  * and there is no single honest label for both.
 607:  *
 608:  * So the key's ledger entry is `real` over a CLOSED ENUMERATION of backed method names, and every
 609:  * method outside that enumeration is a callable proxy that THROWS `CapabilityPending` naming
 610:  * `sut.<key>.<method>` the moment a body touches it.
 611:  *
 612:  * WHY THIS IS NOT A LEDGER STAND-IN, which is the distinction the whole design turns on. A stand-in
 613:  * ANSWERS: it produces a value the test then asserts on, so a suite can go green over it, which is
 614:  * why `registry.ts` refuses one above the loop tier. A pending proxy answers NOTHING â€” it fakes no
 615:  * behaviour, returns no value and can never produce a green. Its only possible effect is to turn one
 616:  * id red, by name, with a detail the declaration machinery can match exactly. The failure direction
 617:  * is a false RED and never a false green, so it belongs on the permitted side of the gate.
 618:  *
 619:  * READ, NEVER CALLED, IS STILL A REFUSAL, and deliberately so. The trap throws from `get`, not from
 620:  * the returned function, so `const f = sut.somethingUnbacked` is already the refusal. A body that
 621:  * merely names an unbacked method has leaned on it, and letting the read pass would move the red to
 622:  * whichever later line happened to invoke it â€” or hide it entirely behind an `if`.
 623:  *
 624:  * THREE PROPERTY READS ARE ANSWERED RATHER THAN REFUSED, and each is a real hazard rather than a
 625:  * convenience: `then` (an `await` on this object probes it, and throwing there turns an unrelated
 626:  * await into this refusal), and the two well-known symbols a runtime reaches for when it prints or
 627:  * inspects a value. A test never asserts on any of the three.
 628:  */
 629: export function pendingMethodProxy<T extends object>(
 630:   capabilityName: string,
 631:   backedMethods: readonly string[],
 632:   surface: Record<string, unknown>,
 633: ): T {
 634:   const backed = new Set(backedMethods);
 635:   return new Proxy(surface, {
 636:     get(target, property, receiver) {
 637:       if (typeof property === 'symbol') return Reflect.get(target, property, receiver) as unknown;
 638:       if (property === 'then') return undefined;
 639:       if (backed.has(property)) {
 640:         const member = Reflect.get(target, property, receiver) as unknown;
 641:         return typeof member === 'function' ? (member as (...args: unknown[]) => unknown).bind(target) : member;
 642:       }
 643:       throw new CapabilityPending([`${capabilityName}.${property}`]);
 644:     },
 645:     /*
 646:      * A BODY CANNOT DISCOVER WHICH METHODS ARE BACKED AND THEN TAKE A DIFFERENT PATH.
 647:      *
 648:      * This trap used to be `Reflect.has(target, property)` while its own comment claimed the
 649:      * behaviour below (gate-2 ruling S1-4). For a contract method the raw adapter OMITS entirely â€”
 650:      * which is every unbacked method, because the live adapter deliberately writes none of them â€”
 651:      * `'method' in sut` answered false, so a body could branch around the refusal and report a
 652:      * green for a criterion it skipped. That is the one thing this proxy exists to prevent.
 653:      *
 654:      * SO EVERY STRING PROPERTY ANSWERS PRESENT, and the read then throws `CapabilityPending` naming
 655:      * it. The failure direction stays a false RED: nothing new can be reached, one more thing
 656:      * refuses. Symbols keep `Reflect.has`, for the same reason the `get` trap answers them â€” a
 657:      * runtime probing for a well-known symbol is not a body leaning on a capability.
 658:      */
 659:     has(target, property) {
 660:       if (typeof property === 'symbol') return Reflect.has(target, property);
 661:       return true;
 662:     },
 663:   }) as T;
 664: }

