 474:     /*
 475:      * TWO RELABELLINGS, AND EXACTLY WHAT EACH ONE STILL TAKES ON TRUST.
 476:      *
 477:      * `AtHarness` describes the LOOP tier's shapes, because that is the tier every existing suite is
 478:      * written against and widening the shared type would let a loop body reach for a member only the
 479:      * integration tier supplies. Above loop the ledger really holds an `AttestedRealClock` (no
 480:      * control seam) and a live mail catcher (no arming methods) â€” both NARROWER than what this type
 481:      * names, which is why the assignment needs saying out loud rather than being silently allowed.
 482:      *
 483:      * NOTHING IS WIDENED BY IT. `registry.ts` hands a body `TierHarness<T>`, which SUBTRACTS the two
 484:      * members at integration, so an integration body that reaches for `advance` or `rejectNext`
 485:      * fails to compile. What the cast buys is one shared factory instead of two, and what it costs
 486:      * is that this one line is the place the two tiers' shapes are reconciled â€” so it is the line to
 487:      * read when they disagree.
 488:      */
 489:     clock: ledger.clock.value as AtHarness['clock'],
 490:     fixtures: ledger.fixtures.value,
 491:     sut: Object.fromEntries(ledger.sut.map((entry) => [entry.name.slice(SUT_PREFIX.length), entry.value] as const)),
 492:     sentinels: ledger.sentinels.value,
